Encapsulation:
--------------

It is the mechanism of wrapping data(variables) and methods(code) together into a single unit. It restricts direct access to some of an objects components. This is achieved by providing private to the data and public getter and setter methods.Encapsulation is about restricting access from OUTSIDE the class, not inside the class.

Why Encapsulation is Important (Interview Keywords)

✔ Data hiding
✔ Security
✔ Controlled access
✔ Maintainability
✔ Flexibility
✔ Loose coupling

How Encapsulation is Achieved in Java
--------------------------------------
Make variables private
Provide public getter and setter methods
Validate data inside setters

**Encapsulation is about restricting access from OUTSIDE the class, not inside the class.**
=============================================================================================================================

Q1. Is Encapsulation mandatory to use getters and setters?
No. Encapsulation means controlled access, not necessarily getters/setters.

Q2. Can a class with all public variables be encapsulated?
❌ No. Data hiding is missing.

Q3. Can we have encapsulation without abstraction?
✅ Yes. They are independent concepts.

Q4. Is Encapsulation compile-time or runtime?
Compile-time (access modifiers checked at compile time).

Q5. Can encapsulation improve performance?
❌ No direct performance gain, but improves maintainability and safety.

Q6. What happens if setter is private?
Object becomes read-only from outside → still encapsulated.

Q7. Why fields should not be public in Java Beans?
Breaks encapsulation, validation, and future flexibility.

====================================================================================================================================================
class Employee {
   private  int age;
   public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }

    public void setAge(int age) {
        if (age > 0 && age < 60) {
            this.age = age;
        } else {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid age");
        }
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee emp = new Employee();
        emp.setAge(30);
        System.out.println(emp.getAge());
    }
}

OUTPUT:- 30

🔑 Key Point
Even though you change age inside the class, encapsulation is still achieved because:
   Access is controlled
   Validation logic exists
   External code cannot directly modify age

4. Addressing a Very Common Interview Confusion 🔥
❓ Question: “Age is private, but I’m still changing it using setter. How is encapsulation achieved?”

✅ Correct Answer:
Encapsulation does not mean data cannot change.
It means data cannot be changed directly without control.

✔ Change happens through:
Business rules
Validation
Controlled access

❌ Without encapsulation:
person.age = -10; // No control ❌

✔ With encapsulation:
person.setAge(-10); // Blocked by validation ✅

5. Encapsulation vs Data Hiding (Tricky)
| Encapsulation             | Data Hiding           |
| ------------------------- | --------------------- |
| Concept                   | Technique             |
| Bundling + access control | Restrict access       |
| Uses getters/setters      | Uses access modifiers |

👉 Encapsulation includes data hiding, but data hiding alone is not encapsulation.

6. Access Modifiers & Encapsulation
| Modifier  | Visibility         |
| --------- | ------------------ |
| private   | Same class only    |
| default   | Same package       |
| protected | Package + subclass |
| public    | Everywhere         |


🔑 Best Practice:
Variables → private
Methods → public (only required ones)

9. Encapsulation & JVM Memory
   Private variables stored in heap
   Access control enforced at compile time
   JVM ensures access only via permitted methods
   Method calls resolved via reference
